PG.notes("l075", "<p class=\"summary\">A history-and-context lecture on JavaScript: why it was created, what 'script' means, how it differs from Java, what interpreted vs compiled means, and why it is the core language of the web.</p>\n<h3>Why JavaScript exists</h3>\n<ul><li>Early web pages were static HTML. Any calculation, lookup or unit conversion needed a round trip to a server that returned a fresh page.</li><li>Netscape wanted pages that react instantly (animation, live interaction), which requires code running inside the browser itself. They commissioned Brendan Eich to build a small, beginner-friendly scripting language, and a first version reportedly took about ten days.</li><li>Browsers are heavily dependent on it: with JavaScript switched off in Chrome settings, sites like Twitter lose features such as the live character counter, ads vanish on news sites, and apps like YouTube or Netflix fail to load.</li></ul>\n<h3>Names and standards</h3>\n<ul><li>The first name was LiveScript. Microsoft's reverse-engineered version was JScript. Differences between versions led to standardisation as ECMAScript (from the European computer manufacturers' association).</li><li>Version names such as ES6 or ES7 refer to ECMAScript editions. ES6 (2015) is the baseline modern syntax this section teaches.</li></ul>\n<h3>What 'script' means</h3>\n<ul><li>A script is a list of instructions for actors; in a web page the actors are HTML elements and the script is JavaScript.</li><li>Example idea: unhide the h1, wait one second, unhide the paragraph, then change the h1 text to Hello and the paragraph text to World. This shows JavaScript reading and changing elements over time.</li></ul>\n<h3>JavaScript vs Java</h3>\n<ul><li>They are unrelated languages; the name was a 1990s marketing choice because 'Java' was fashionable.</li><li>JavaScript is interpreted: the engine runs instructions as it reads them. Java is compiled: translated to a runnable form ahead of time. Interpreted languages used to be seen as slow toys, but modern engines are fast and capable.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Modern JavaScript engines use just-in-time compilation, so the interpreted/compiled split is blurrier than it was.</li></ul>\n<h3>Where it is used and why learn it first</h3>\n<ul><li>Originally front-end only; now also used in back-end and full-stack frameworks, which later sections cover.</li><li>It is the only language every major browser runs natively. Other languages can compile to JavaScript, but JavaScript remains the foundation.</li><li>It topped the RedMonk language popularity ranking in early 2018, narrowly ahead of Java.</li><li>Choose a language by target: Swift for iOS, Java (or Kotlin) for Android, JavaScript for websites and web apps. Its quirks can bite, so the following lessons stress good practice.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Assuming JavaScript and Java are related or share syntax rules and behaviour.</li><li>Calling versions 'JS6'; the standard naming is ES6 or ECMAScript 2015.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-alerts", "<p class=\"summary\">First hands-on JavaScript: running alert() in Chrome's DevTools console and Snippets editor, reading a ReferenceError, looking things up on MDN, and learning the grammar (function name, parentheses, string quotes, semicolon) plus style conventions.</p>\n<h3>Two places to run JavaScript in Chrome</h3>\n<ul><li>DevTools Console (View &gt; Developer &gt; JavaScript Console, or Cmd+Option+J / Ctrl+Shift+J): executes each line the moment Enter is pressed. Good for testing one-liners.</li><li>In the console, Shift+Enter inserts a new line without running, needed for multi-line input. Forgetting it and pressing Enter loses the half-written code.</li><li>Sources tab &gt; left-hand panel arrow &gt; Snippets: create a snippet (+ New snippet, e.g. index.js), write many lines, then run the whole file with the run button at the bottom right (or Cmd/Ctrl+Enter). This works like a mini editor and is what the next lessons use for exercises.</li><li>Output from the run appears in the console; each statement runs in order, and a dialog must be dismissed before the next line executes.</li></ul>\n<h3>The alert function</h3>\n<ul><li>alert(&quot;Hello&quot;); shows a pop-up with that message and an OK button. It is a function: a built-in piece of behaviour the browser knows how to perform.</li><li>Anatomy: the function name (alert), parentheses holding the argument (the message), quotation marks marking text as a string rather than code, and a semicolon ending the instruction.</li><li>alert is a method of the global window object, so window.alert(&quot;Hello&quot;); is the long form. The short form is standard practice.</li><li>The message can be any text; the browser does not interpret it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Errors and finding valid keywords</h3>\n<ul><li>Calling an unknown name, such as say(&quot;Hello&quot;), stops the script with Uncaught ReferenceError: say is not defined. It means the browser has no function or variable with that name.</li><li>Code before the faulty line still ran; execution halts at the error.</li><li>MDN Web Docs (JavaScript section) is the reference for valid keywords and syntax. Searching 'alert' leads to Window.alert(), which shows the syntax, an example and the result.</li></ul>\n<h3>Syntax is grammar</h3>\n<ul><li>Semicolon is like a full stop: it ends one instruction so the browser does not merge it with the next line.</li><li>Quotes mark a string; the contents are text, not code.</li><li>Parentheses hold what the function should receive.</li><li>Different languages (Ruby, Python, Java) use different symbols for the same ideas.</li></ul>\n<h3>Style conventions</h3>\n<ul><li>Use straight code quotes, never typographic 'curly' quotes. Word processors and note apps substitute curly ones, which break code when pasted. Code editors insert the correct ones.</li><li>No spaces between the function name, parentheses, quotes and semicolon: alert(&quot;Hello&quot;);. Extra spaces work but are not the convention.</li><li>Single or double quotes behave identically for strings; the common convention for text is double quotes. (She notes single quotes are preferred in some situations later.)</li><li>A JavaScript style guide (she points to a community-maintained one) states the goal: code should look as though one person wrote it. Bookmark one and revisit it as you gain experience.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Two alerts in a snippet; the second shows after the first is dismissed</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">alert(&quot;Hello&quot;);\nalert(&quot;World&quot;);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Unknown keyword causes a ReferenceError</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">say(&quot;Hello&quot;);\n// Uncaught ReferenceError: say is not defined</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo's 14.01 script.js (short and long form)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">alert(&quot;Hello World&quot;);\nwindow.alert(&quot;Olá Mundo&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Pasting code from Word, Notes or web pages that contain curly quotes.</li><li>Pressing Enter instead of Shift+Enter when writing multi-line code in the console.</li><li>Misspelling a built-in name (Alert vs alert); JavaScript is case-sensitive.</li><li>Leaving out the closing quote or parenthesis.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-data-types", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces data types: string, number and boolean. Shows how quotes mark a string, how the console evaluates arithmetic, and how typeof reports a value's type.</p>\n<h3>Why types exist</h3>\n<ul><li>Code is itself written as text, so the computer needs a way to tell code apart from plain text data. Quotation marks do this: everything inside them is a string, not instructions.</li><li>A data type classifies a value so the program knows how it may be used. Strings, numbers and booleans appear in virtually all languages.</li><li>String = a sequence of characters (text that is not meant to be run as code).</li></ul>\n<h3>The three basic types</h3>\n<ul><li>String: written inside quotes, e.g. &quot;Hello&quot;.</li><li>Number: written bare, with no quotes (23, 3.14). Digits do not clash with code keywords, so no marker is needed.</li><li>Boolean: true or false, written bare in lowercase, no quotes.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Putting quotes around a number, like &quot;23&quot;, makes it a string, which changes how + and other operators behave.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Other types exist (undefined, null, object, bigint, symbol); the playground lesson covers some.</li></ul>\n<h3>Trying it in the console</h3>\n<ul><li>Type 2 + 3 and press Enter: the console evaluates it and prints 5.</li><li>alert(2 + 3); shows a pop-up containing 5, because the expression is evaluated before alert receives it.</li><li>typeof reports a value's type as a string: typeof 23 gives &quot;number&quot;, typeof &quot;a name&quot; gives &quot;string&quot;, typeof true gives &quot;boolean&quot;. It is shown with parentheses in the lecture, typeof(23), but works as an operator without them.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Checking types (matches the reference repo's 14.02 script)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">alert(typeof 18);       // &quot;number&quot;\nalert(typeof &quot;Wesley&quot;); // &quot;string&quot;\nalert(typeof true);     // &quot;boolean&quot;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Arithmetic is evaluated before the alert shows</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">alert(2 + 3); // pop-up says 5</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Quoting a number or boolean turns it into a string.</li><li>Writing True or TRUE; the boolean literals are lowercase.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-variables", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces variables as named containers for data, using var. Shows prompt() for user input, why unsaved input is lost, reassigning a variable, using a variable inside alert, and combining strings and variables with +.</p>\n<h3>Why variables</h3>\n<ul><li>A variable stores a value in the computer's memory under a name so it can be reused without retyping or re-asking for it.</li><li>Without storing, data from a prompt is gone as soon as the dialog closes.</li></ul>\n<h3>prompt()</h3>\n<ul><li>prompt(&quot;What is your name?&quot;); looks like alert but also shows a text field. The user types text and clicks OK.</li><li>The call evaluates to the entered text (a string), so it can be assigned to a variable. (Extra) Cancel returns null, and numbers typed in still arrive as strings.</li><li>Run a snippet with Cmd/Ctrl+Enter or the run button in Chrome DevTools &gt; Sources &gt; Snippets.</li></ul>\n<h3>Declaring and assigning with var</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: var myName = &quot;Angela&quot;; The keyword var creates a new container, myName is its label, = places the value on the right into it, and the semicolon ends the statement.</li><li>After running the snippet, typing myName in the Console returns the stored string, proving it stays in memory.</li><li>Reassign by writing the name alone: myName = &quot;Jimmy&quot;; The old value is replaced. Do not repeat var; it is used only once, when the variable is first created.</li><li>Referring to a variable (no quotes) retrieves its value. alert(myName); shows the value; alert(&quot;myName&quot;); would show the literal word.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The course uses var here; modern code prefers let and const (the playground teaches those). var is function-scoped and can be redeclared, which causes bugs.</li></ul>\n<h3>Combining text and variables</h3>\n<ul><li>The + operator joins strings and variable values into one message: alert(&quot;My name is &quot; + myName + &quot;, welcome to my course &quot; + yourName + &quot;!&quot;);</li><li>Spaces and punctuation must be placed inside the quoted pieces, because + adds nothing between them.</li><li>Variables hold changing state: a gameLevel variable can start at 1, be updated to 2 and 3 as the player progresses, and be shown with alert(&quot;Your level is currently: &quot; + gameLevel);.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Store prompt input in a variable and reuse it</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var myName = &quot;Angela&quot;;\nvar yourName = prompt(&quot;What is your name?&quot;);\nalert(&quot;My name is &quot; + myName + &quot;, welcome to my course &quot; + yourName + &quot;!&quot;);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reassigning and a changing game level</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var gameLevel = 1;\ngameLevel = 2;\ngameLevel = 3;\nalert(&quot;Your level is currently: &quot; + gameLevel);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Mini challenge: alert the variable without typing a string</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A snippet containing var myName = &quot;Angela&quot;;</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show the contents of myName in an alert without typing the string itself again.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Write alert( ) and place the variable name myName between the parentheses, without quotes.</li><li>Run the snippet to see the stored value appear.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A pop-up showing Angela.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var myName = &quot;Angela&quot;;\nalert(myName);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting quotes around the variable name, which prints the name instead of its value.</li><li>Repeating var when you only want to change the value.</li><li>Missing spaces inside concatenated strings, so words run together.</li><li>Calling prompt() without assigning it, so the input is lost.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l079", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the next lesson's coding exercise: swap the values held by two variables under three constraints. The solution is explained in the following lecture.</p>\n<h3>How the exercise is presented</h3>\n<ul><li>The next lesson is an in-browser coding exercise with instructions on top and an editable code file below, plus a linked online sandbox (Repl.it) for experimenting. Check Solution validates the answer; Continue moves on to the walkthrough.</li><li>Starting code declares a as 3 and b as 8 and logs them. Running it unchanged prints a is 3 and b is 8.</li><li>A mental model: two buckets, one with sand and one with water; how do you exchange the contents?</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Starting situation, reconstructed</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var a = 3;\nvar b = 8;\n\n// your three lines go here\n\nconsole.log(&quot;a is &quot; + a);\nconsole.log(&quot;b is &quot; + b);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Swap two variables</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Two variables: a holds 3, b holds 8. Three blank lines (7 to 9) sit between the declarations and the logging.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> After your code, a must hold 8 and b must hold 3.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Do not modify any existing line; add code only in the three free lines.</li><li>Do not type any numeric literals (so no a = 8 or b = 3).</li><li>Do not redeclare a or b with var/let/const.</li><li>Use the online sandbox to test, then enter the answer and press Check Solution.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Console output reads a is 8 and b is 3.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Assigning a = b then b = a loses the original value of a, so both end up 8. A third holder is needed (see the solution lecture).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l080", "<p class=\"summary\">Walkthrough of the variable-swap exercise: introduce a third variable as temporary storage, then reassign in three steps so a and b trade values without literals or redeclaration.</p>\n<h3>Why the rules exist</h3>\n<ul><li>The constraints test understanding of how variables hold and replace values. Swap puzzles like this are common in coding interviews to observe how a candidate reasons.</li><li>Editing the given 3 and 8 is not a solution; typing a = 8 and b = 3 uses literals and is not a solution; writing var a = 8 redeclares the names and the editor flags 'already defined'.</li><li>Real-life reasoning helps: to exchange the contents of two buckets you need a third, empty bucket. The hard part of coding is working out the logic, not remembering syntax.</li></ul>\n<h3>The three-step swap</h3>\n<ul><li>Declare a new variable c and copy a's value into it: var c = a; (c now holds 3).</li><li>Overwrite a with b's value: a = b; (a is 8; the original 3 is safe in c).</li><li>Overwrite b with c: b = c; (b is 3).</li><li>Only c is newly declared, so no existing variable is redeclared, and no number literals are typed.</li><li>Order matters: copying b into a first would destroy a's original value.</li></ul>\n<h3>Verifying</h3>\n<ul><li>Running the code logs a is 8 and b is 3; Check Solution then passes.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Modern JavaScript can swap without a temp via destructuring: [a, b] = [b, a]; (needs a preceding semicolon on the previous line).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution lines (inserted between the existing declarations and the logging)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var a = 3;\nvar b = 8;\n\n// --- solution ---\nvar c = a;\na = b;\nb = c;\n// ----------------\n\nconsole.log(&quot;a is &quot; + a);   // a is 8\nconsole.log(&quot;b is &quot; + b);   // b is 3</pre>\n<h2>The task: Swap two variables (solution)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> a = 3, b = 8 with three blank lines.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> a becomes 8 and b becomes 3 with no number literals, no redeclaration of a or b, and no edits to existing code.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>var c = a;  store a's value.</li><li>a = b;  copy b into a.</li><li>b = c;  copy the stored value into b.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> a is 8 and b is 3.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var c = a;\na = b;\nb = c;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>a = b; b = a; leaves both variables at 8.</li><li>Reusing var for a or b creates a redeclaration warning.</li><li>Hard-coding the numbers passes the output but breaks the rules.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-naming", "<p class=\"summary\">Rules and conventions for naming variables: which names JavaScript rejects, what characters are allowed, why meaningful names matter, and camelCase. Also shows how to clear the DevTools console versus truly resetting memory.</p>\n<h3>Clearing the console versus clearing state</h3>\n<ul><li>Cmd+K (Mac) or Ctrl+K (Windows) clears the console output only. Variables created earlier still exist, so typing myName still returns its value.</li><li>To wipe stored state, do a hard reload: hold the reload button (Mac) or right-click it (Windows) and choose Empty Cache and Hard Reload. Afterwards the old variable is gone (typing it gives a not-defined error).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A plain page refresh in DevTools usually also discards variables from the page's memory, since a fresh page context is created.</li></ul>\n<h3>Hard rules (JavaScript enforces these)</h3>\n<ul><li>Cannot be a reserved word: var var = ... is an error. A name may contain a keyword (myvar is fine).</li><li>Cannot start with a digit: 123 is invalid, but my123 is valid.</li><li>No spaces: my name would be read as variable my followed by a stray token.</li><li>Only letters, digits, $ and _ are allowed. my-name is invalid because the hyphen is read as subtraction, but my_name is valid.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Names are case-sensitive: myname, myName and MyName are three different variables.</li></ul>\n<h3>Conventions (humans enforce these)</h3>\n<ul><li>Choose meaningful names that make the contents obvious; strings of random characters work but make shared code unreadable. Compare folders labelled Pictures or Documents with folders called untitled folder 1 to 6.</li><li>Use camelCase: first word lowercase, each following word capitalised (userScoreFinal). Capital letters mark word boundaries so names stay readable.</li><li>A website domain example (a therapist-finder site) shows how running words together without case can be misread.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Valid and invalid names (matches the reference repo 14.04 file)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var myName = &quot;Wesley&quot;;\nvar my123 = 123;      // digits allowed, but not at the start\nvar myvar = &quot;x&quot;;      // contains a keyword: fine\nvar my_name = &quot;Eve&quot;;  // underscore ok\nvar userScoreFinal = 12; // camelCase\n\n// var var = 1;       // error: reserved word\n// var 123 = 1;       // error: starts with a number\n// var my name = 1;   // error: space\n// var my-name = 1;   // error: hyphen</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using hyphens in names (kebab-case works in CSS, not JavaScript).</li><li>Starting a name with a number.</li><li>Thinking the console clear command also clears variables.</li><li>Assuming any name that runs is a good name.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-concat", "<p class=\"summary\">Concatenation joins strings (and string variables) into one string using +. Only the text inside quotes is combined, so spaces must be added explicitly. Ends with a greeting challenge using two variables.</p>\n<h3>Concatenation with +</h3>\n<ul><li>Concatenation means joining strings end to end into a single new string. With strings, + does this rather than arithmetic.</li><li>Only the characters within the quotes are joined: &quot;a&quot; + &quot; &quot; + &quot;b&quot; gives &quot;a b&quot;.</li><li>alert(&quot;hello&quot; + &quot;world&quot;); shows helloworld with no gap.</li><li>Add the space either as its own string (&quot;hello&quot; + &quot; &quot; + &quot;world&quot;) or inside one of the pieces (&quot;hello&quot; + &quot; world&quot;).</li><li>Variables holding strings can be mixed with literals in the same expression, e.g. message + &quot; &quot; + name.</li></ul>\n<h3>Greeting challenge walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Create var message = &quot;Hello&quot;; and var name set either to a fixed name or to prompt(&quot;What is your name?&quot;).</li><li>Write one alert that concatenates message, a space string and name.</li><li>The key detail is the space between the two variables; without it the output reads HelloAngela.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The name for the second variable can clash in browsers: name is already a property of window, which always stores strings. Using a different name such as userName avoids surprises.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Concatenation basics</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">alert(&quot;hello&quot; + &quot;world&quot;);   // helloworld\nalert(&quot;hello&quot; + &quot; &quot; + &quot;world&quot;); // hello world\nalert(&quot;hello&quot; + &quot; world&quot;);      // hello world</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Challenge solution (reference repo 14.05)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var message = &quot;Hello&quot;;\nvar name = prompt(&quot;What is your name?&quot;);\nalert(message + &quot; &quot; + name);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Greeting with concatenation</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty snippet in DevTools Sources &gt; Snippets.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show a greeting built from two variables, not a typed-out string.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Declare message with the value &quot;Hello&quot;.</li><li>Declare name as your own name or the result of prompt().</li><li>Write a single alert that joins message, a space string and name with +.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A pop-up reading Hello followed by the name, separated by a space.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var message = &quot;Hello&quot;;\nvar name = prompt(&quot;What is your name?&quot;);\nalert(message + &quot; &quot; + name);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting spaces, so words run together.</li><li>Putting a variable name in quotes, which prints the name instead of the value.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Using + with a number and a string makes a string (&quot;7&quot; + 1 is &quot;71&quot;).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-length", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the .length property of strings, JavaScript comments (// and /* */), and a tweet-character-counter challenge that combines prompt, length, arithmetic and concatenation.</p>\n<h3>The length property</h3>\n<ul><li>stringVariable.length returns the number of characters in the string. With var name = &quot;Angela&quot;; name.length is 6.</li><li>It is a property (no parentheses). It can be used on a variable or directly on a literal.</li><li>Every character counts, including spaces and punctuation.</li><li>In a snippet, evaluating name.length on its own prints 6 in the console.</li></ul>\n<h3>Comments</h3>\n<ul><li>// turns the rest of that line into a comment; the engine ignores it. The next line is code again.</li><li>/* ... */ wraps a multi-line comment of any length.</li><li>Useful for temporarily disabling code and for leaving notes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Tweet counter challenge</h3>\n<ul><li>Goal: let the user paste text, then report how many characters were written and how many remain against a limit (140 historically, 280 now).</li><li>Steps: prompt for the text and store it in a variable (tweet); store tweet.length in tweetCount; build an alert with concatenation: written count, then the limit minus the count.</li><li>The subtraction must be wrapped in parentheses inside the concatenation, so it is calculated as a number before joining. Going over the limit gives a negative remainder (e.g. -47).</li><li>The tweetCount variable is optional; using tweet.length directly in both places works the same. The extra variable only makes the logic clearer.</li></ul>\n<h3>Common error: length of null</h3>\n<ul><li>If the prompt is dismissed (for example by switching browser tabs, which closes the dialog), the variable becomes null, and reading .length raises 'Cannot read property length of null' (newer browsers word it 'Cannot read properties of null (reading length)').</li><li>Always give the prompt a value (or Press OK) before the line using it. Null, undefined and NaN are covered in later lessons.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Tweet counter (reconstructed; reference repo uses 280)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var tweet = prompt(&quot;Compose your tweet&quot;);\nvar tweetCount = tweet.length;\nalert(&quot;You have written &quot; + tweetCount + &quot; characters, you have &quot; + (140 - tweetCount) + &quot; characters remaining.&quot;);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo 14.06 (author's version, typos fixed)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var text = prompt(&quot;Write a text with a max of 280 characters.&quot;);\nvar charactersRemaining = 280 - text.length;\nalert(&quot;You have written &quot; + text.length + &quot; characters, you have &quot; + charactersRemaining + &quot; characters left.&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Twitter character counter</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty Chrome snippet.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Prompt for a long text and alert how many characters were written and how many are left from a 140 (or 280) limit.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Prompt the user for text and save it in a variable.</li><li>Use .length to get the character count.</li><li>Compute limit minus count (parentheses needed if done inside the alert).</li><li>Alert a sentence that joins the pieces with +.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> For 187 characters with a 140 limit: 'You have written 187 characters, you have -47 characters remaining.'</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var tweet = prompt(&quot;Compose your tweet&quot;);\nvar tweetCount = tweet.length;\nalert(&quot;You have written &quot; + tweetCount + &quot; characters, you have &quot; + (140 - tweetCount) + &quot; characters remaining.&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing .length() with parentheses.</li><li>Leaving out the parentheses around 140 - tweetCount: &quot;...&quot; + 140 - tweetCount makes a string minus a number (NaN).</li><li>Dismissing the prompt then calling .length on null.</li><li>Reference repo has misspellings of 'characters' in its alert text.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-slice", "<p class=\"summary\">Teaches string.slice(start, end): zero-based positions, an exclusive end index, and how to compute the result length. The exercise truncates a tweet to 140 characters, with a condensed one-expression version.</p>\n<h3>slice(start, end)</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: string.slice(startIndex, endIndex). It must be called on a string or a variable holding one (name.slice(0, 3)); slice alone is meaningless.</li><li>Returns a new string; the original is unchanged. (Extra) That is true of all string methods because strings are immutable.</li><li>Indices count from 0, so in &quot;Angela&quot; A is position 0 and the last letter a is position 5 (the sixth character).</li><li>The start index is included, the end index is excluded: slice(0, 1) gives &quot;A&quot;, slice(5, 6) gives &quot;a&quot;, slice(0, 3) gives &quot;Ang&quot;.</li><li>Quick length check: end minus start gives the number of characters returned (3 - 0 = 3; 5 - 1 = 4).</li><li>On &quot;abc&quot;, slice(1, 3) returns &quot;bc&quot; because positions 1 and 2 are included and position 3 does not exist.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> If the end index is omitted, slice goes to the end of the string; negative numbers count back from the end; an end beyond the length is clamped, so a short text is not padded or errored.</li></ul>\n<h3>Editor shortcut</h3>\n<ul><li>Cmd+/ (Mac) or Ctrl+/ (Windows) toggles comment on the selected lines, handy for switching old code off and on in the snippets editor.</li></ul>\n<h3>Enforcing a tweet limit</h3>\n<ul><li>Instead of only reporting excess characters, cut the text down to the limit.</li><li>Prompt for text, then slice from 0 to 140 (140 - 0 = 140 characters).</li><li>A variable name can include digits but not start with one, hence tweetUnder140.</li><li>Verify with .length on the result.</li><li>Variables here are only for readability: the whole thing collapses to a single alert(prompt(&quot;...&quot;).slice(0, 140)) expression because a function call can stand in for the value it returns.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">slice examples</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var name = &quot;Angela&quot;;\nname.slice(0, 1); // &quot;A&quot;\nname.slice(5, 6); // &quot;a&quot;\nname.slice(0, 3); // &quot;Ang&quot;\nname.slice(1, 5); // &quot;ngel&quot; (5 - 1 = 4 characters)</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Tweet truncation, step by step</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var tweet = prompt(&quot;Compose your tweet:&quot;);\nvar tweetUnder140 = tweet.slice(0, 140);\nalert(tweetUnder140);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Condensed version (same idea as reference repo 14.07, which limits to 10 characters)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">alert(prompt(&quot;Compose your tweet:&quot;).slice(0, 140));</pre>\n<h2>The task: Cut a tweet down to 140 characters</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Snippet containing the prompt for a tweet (from the earlier counter exercise).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Alert back only the first 140 characters of whatever was entered.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Prompt for text and keep it in a variable, tweet.</li><li>Create tweetUnder140 = tweet.slice(0, 140).</li><li>Alert tweetUnder140.</li><li>Test with a long pasted paragraph and check the result with .length.</li><li>Optional: merge into one line by replacing variables with the expressions they hold.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> An alert showing exactly the first 140 characters of the input.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var tweet = prompt(&quot;Compose your tweet:&quot;);\nvar tweetUnder140 = tweet.slice(0, 140);\nalert(tweetUnder140);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Counting from 1 instead of 0.</li><li>Expecting the end index to be included.</li><li>Calling slice without a string in front of it.</li><li>Null from a dismissed prompt makes .slice throw an error.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-casing", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces toUpperCase() and toLowerCase() for changing the case of a string, shows that the result must be reassigned to persist, then sets a challenge: prompt for a name and alert a greeting where only the first letter is capitalised.</p>\n<h3>toUpperCase() and toLowerCase()</h3>\n<ul><li>Both are string methods called with dot notation on a variable: name.toUpperCase() returns the string with every character in capitals; name.toLowerCase() returns it in small letters.</li><li>The parentheses are required even though no argument is passed, and the statement ends with a semicolon.</li><li>In the console demo the uppercase string is printed, but checking name afterwards still shows the original lowercase text.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Strings are immutable in JavaScript, so these methods never edit the original; they return a new string.</li></ul>\n<h3>Keeping the changed value</h3>\n<ul><li>To keep the result, reassign it to the same variable: name = name.toUpperCase();</li><li>Reassigning with name = name.toLowerCase(); later lowercases the whole value, including the first letter that had been capitalised.</li><li>Typing the variable name in the console after running shows its current value, which is a quick way to verify a reassignment worked.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why casing matters</h3>\n<ul><li>Users type input inconsistently (all lowercase, all caps, mixed). Normalising case before printing makes output such as an alert look consistent across users.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Changing case and reassigning</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var name = &quot;Angela&quot;;\nname.toUpperCase();          // returns &quot;ANGELA&quot; but name is unchanged\nname = name.toUpperCase();   // now name holds &quot;ANGELA&quot;\nname = name.toLowerCase();   // now name holds &quot;angela&quot;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Capitalise the first letter of a name</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty script in the editor (reference repo: index.html linking script.js).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Ask the user for their name with a prompt and show an alert greeting them, with only the first character capitalised and the rest lowercase, regardless of how it was typed.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Use prompt() to ask for the name and store it in a variable.</li><li>Slice out the first character with slice(0, 1).</li><li>Slice out the remainder from index 1 to the end.</li><li>Uppercase the first part and lowercase the remainder.</li><li>Concatenate a greeting, the capitalised letter and the remainder, then pass it to alert().</li><li>Constraint: use only techniques from this module (slice, concatenation, case methods), not Stack Overflow shortcuts.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Entering aNGELA, ANGELA or angela all produce an alert reading Hello Angela.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var name = prompt(&quot;What is your first name?&quot;);\nvar capitalLetter = name.slice(0, 1);\nvar remainderOfName = name.slice(1, name.length);\nalert(&quot;Hello &quot; + capitalLetter.toUpperCase() + remainderOfName.toLowerCase());</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Calling name.toUpperCase() without assigning the result leaves the variable unchanged.</li><li>Forgetting the parentheses turns the call into a reference to the method rather than running it.</li><li>Reference repo page has an author footer; ignore it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l086", "<p class=\"summary\">Walks through the capitalise-the-first-letter challenge by decomposing it into small steps, checking each variable in the console, then adding one more step so odd input like angELA still comes out correctly.</p>\n<h3>Problem decomposition</h3>\n<ul><li>The main goal is a skill rather than the code: split a large problem into pieces, and split any piece that still feels hard.</li><li>Top-level plan: (1) store the prompted name, (2) capitalise its first letter, (3) greet the user in an alert.</li><li>Step 2 turned out to be too big and was split into: isolate first character, uppercase it, isolate the remainder, join them.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Simpler one-liners exist on Stack Overflow, but the exercise deliberately restricts you to the module's tools.</li></ul>\n<h3>Verifying each step in the console</h3>\n<ul><li>Run each line in the browser console as it is written and then type the variable name to confirm its value (for example the first character is a lowercase a).</li><li>This incremental checking catches mistakes early instead of debugging a finished block.</li></ul>\n<h3>Slicing the first character and the rest</h3>\n<ul><li>name.slice(0, 1) starts at position 0 and stops before position 1, giving just the first character.</li><li>name.slice(1, name.length) starts after the first character and runs to the end.</li><li>Hard-coding an end index (such as 6 for Angela) only works for names of that exact length, so use the length property, which adapts to whatever the user enters.</li><li>Uppercase the isolated first character with .toUpperCase() (parentheses required) and join with + concatenation.</li></ul>\n<h3>Edge case: capital letters later in the name</h3>\n<ul><li>Input like a lowercase start with capitals near the end (e.g. angELA) leaves the tail in capitals with the first version of the solution.</li><li>Fix by adding a step that reassigns the remainder: restOfName = restOfName.toLowerCase();</li><li>Lesson: test with unusual input, because real users will supply it.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Step-by-step solution with the edge case handled</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var name = prompt(&quot;What is your first name?&quot;);\n\n// a) isolate the first character\nvar firstChar = name.slice(0, 1);\n// b) upper-case it\nvar upperCaseFirstChar = firstChar.toUpperCase();\n// c) isolate everything after it\nvar restOfName = name.slice(1, name.length);\n// d) lower-case the remainder (guards against input like &quot;angELA&quot;)\nrestOfName = restOfName.toLowerCase();\n// e) join the pieces\nvar capitalisedName = upperCaseFirstChar + restOfName;\n\nalert(&quot;Hello, &quot; + capitalisedName);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Solution walkthrough: capitalised greeting</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty script; the challenge from the previous lecture.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Greet the user with a name whose first letter is uppercase and whose other letters are lowercase.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create name from prompt().</li><li>Create firstChar with name.slice(0, 1).</li><li>Create upperCaseFirstChar with firstChar.toUpperCase().</li><li>Create restOfName with name.slice(1, name.length).</li><li>Reassign restOfName to its toLowerCase() version.</li><li>Concatenate upperCaseFirstChar + restOfName and use it in alert(&quot;Hello, &quot; + ...).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Typing angELA (or any casing) produces an alert saying Hello, Angela.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var name = prompt(&quot;What is your first name?&quot;);\n\n// a) isolate the first character\nvar firstChar = name.slice(0, 1);\n// b) upper-case it\nvar upperCaseFirstChar = firstChar.toUpperCase();\n// c) isolate everything after it\nvar restOfName = name.slice(1, name.length);\n// d) lower-case the remainder (guards against input like &quot;angELA&quot;)\nrestOfName = restOfName.toLowerCase();\n// e) join the pieces\nvar capitalisedName = upperCaseFirstChar + restOfName;\n\nalert(&quot;Hello, &quot; + capitalisedName);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Hard-coding the slice end index instead of using name.length.</li><li>Not lowercasing the remainder, so mixed-case input keeps stray capitals.</li><li>Forgetting the empty parentheses on the case methods.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Cancelling the prompt returns null, and null.slice throws an error; the lecture does not handle this.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-arithmetic", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the arithmetic operators (+ - * / %), operator precedence and the habit of adding parentheses for clarity, then applies it in a dog-age-to-human-age converter challenge.</p>\n<h3>Arithmetic operators</h3>\n<ul><li>+ adds, - subtracts, * multiplies, / divides.</li><li>% is the modulo (remainder) operator. It looks like division but returns what is left over: 9 % 6 is 3 because 6 fits once into 9; 6 % 4 is 2 (whereas 6 / 4 is 1.5); 12 % 8 is 4.</li></ul>\n<h3>Uses for modulo</h3>\n<ul><li>Odd or even test: n % 2 gives 0 for even numbers and a non-zero value for odd ones (45 % 2 is 1, so 45 is odd).</li><li>More uses come later in the course, such as every-nth-item logic in loops.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The test is written n % 2 === 0 in code.</li></ul>\n<h3>Operator precedence</h3>\n<ul><li>Multiplication and division run before addition and subtraction, same as in school maths. 3 + 5 * 2 evaluates to 13, not 16.</li><li>Parentheses force a different order: (3 + 5) * 2 gives 16.</li><li>Good practice: add parentheses even where precedence already gives the right order, so the intended order is obvious to readers.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Modulo and precedence</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">console.log(9 % 6);        // 3\nconsole.log(6 % 4);        // 2\nconsole.log(12 % 8);       // 4\nconsole.log(45 % 2);       // 1 -&gt; odd\nvar cost = 3 + 5 * 2;      // 13 (multiply first)\nvar cost2 = (3 + 5) * 2;   // 16</pre>\n<h2>The task: Dog age to human age converter</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty script (originally typed in the Chrome DevTools Sources snippet, index.js).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Prompt for a dog's age, convert it with the formula (dogAge - 2) * 4 + 21, and alert the human-equivalent age.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create dogAge from prompt(&quot;How old is your dog?&quot;).</li><li>Compute humanAge: subtract 2, multiply by 4, add 21, grouping with parentheses so the order is explicit.</li><li>Show the result with alert(), concatenating a sentence around humanAge.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Entering 7 gives 41 (that is (7-2)*4+21).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var dogAge = prompt(&quot;How old is your dog?&quot;);\nvar humanAge = ((dogAge - 2) * 4) + 21;\nalert(&quot;If he were human he would be &quot; + humanAge + &quot; years old&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing the formula without parentheses so precedence changes the answer.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> prompt() returns a string; subtraction, multiplication coerce it to a number so the formula works, but dogAge + 2 would concatenate.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's index.html is plain boilerplate; the work is in script.js.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-increment", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows the programming-only shorthand operators ++, --, +=, -=, *= and /= as compact forms of reassigning a variable.</p>\n<h3>Increment and decrement</h3>\n<ul><li>x++ is shorthand for x = x + 1 (increment). With x at 5 it becomes 6.</li><li>x-- is shorthand for x = x - 1 (decrement). With x at 5 it becomes 4.</li><li>These only ever change the value by exactly one.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The name of C++ is a nod to this operator: C plus one.</li></ul>\n<h3>Compound assignment operators</h3>\n<ul><li>x += 2 is the same as x = x + 2; use it to change by more than one.</li><li>The right side can be another variable: x += y means x = x + y.</li><li>The same pattern exists for -=, *= and /=, each applying its operator and storing the result back in the left variable.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Shorthand forms</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var x = 5;\nx++;        // x = x + 1  -&gt; 6\nx--;        // x = x - 1  -&gt; 5\nx += 2;     // x = x + 2  -&gt; 7\nvar y = 1;\nx += y;     // x = x + y  -&gt; 8\nx -= 3;     // 5\nx *= 2;     // 10\nx /= 5;     // 2</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo demo (prefix form and compound assignments)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var a = 5, b = 5, c = 5, d = 5, e = 5;\nconsole.log(a + 1); // 6, a unchanged\nconsole.log(++b);   // 6\nconsole.log(--c);   // 4\nconsole.log(d += 2);// 7\nconsole.log(e -= 2);// 3</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> a + 1 on its own does not change a; only ++, -- and the assignment operators do.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> x++ (postfix) returns the old value while ++x (prefix) returns the new one; matters only when the expression's value is used, as in console.log.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-functions-1", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces functions as named, reusable blocks of instructions, using a house-robot 'get milk' routine; covers the declaration syntax, calling with name plus parentheses, indentation style, and swapping alert for console.log while testing.</p>\n<h3>Why functions exist</h3>\n<ul><li>A fixed sequence of instructions (a robot walking to a shop, buying milk and returning, written as a long list of alert calls) would otherwise have to be retyped every time it is needed.</li><li>A function packages that sequence under a name so one short call repeats the whole thing. This keeps code DRY (do not repeat yourself).</li><li>A computer only does what it is told, so the function must contain every low-level step.</li></ul>\n<h3>Declaring a function</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: the function keyword, then the name, then a pair of round brackets, then curly braces holding the body.</li><li>The opening { marks where the instructions start and the closing } where they end; everything between runs when the function is called.</li><li>The structure parallels a variable: var name = value creates a container, function name() { ... } creates a named block of behaviour.</li><li>Function names follow the variable naming rules and camelCase convention (getMilk); a name cannot start with a digit.</li><li>The brackets are empty here; parameters come in the next lesson.</li><li>Style: indent every line inside the braces so the parent/child structure is visible. A function declaration's closing brace is not followed by a semicolon.</li></ul>\n<h3>Declaring versus calling</h3>\n<ul><li>Declaring defines what the function does and runs nothing.</li><li>Calling is writing getMilk(); : the name, empty parentheses and a semicolon, with no function keyword and no braces.</li><li>On a call the engine finds the matching declaration and executes its body in order.</li></ul>\n<h3>alert versus console.log</h3>\n<ul><li>Clicking through many alerts while testing is tedious; replace them with console.log() (in the editor use find-and-replace, Replace All).</li><li>alert is visible to every visitor of the page. console.log output only appears in the developer console and is meant for debugging and inspecting values.</li><li>The function works identically either way.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">A function with the milk-run instructions, called twice</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function getMilk() {\n    console.log(&quot;leaveHouse&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveRight&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveRight&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveUp&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveUp&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveUp&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveUp&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveRight&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveRight&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;buyMilk&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveLeft&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveLeft&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveDown&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveDown&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveDown&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveDown&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveLeft&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveLeft&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;enterHouse&quot;);\n}\n\ngetMilk();   // runs every instruction above\ngetMilk();   // and again, with no retyping</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing getMilk without parentheses does not run it.</li><li>Starting a function name with a number, or using spaces.</li><li>Putting a semicolon after the closing brace of a declaration is unnecessary.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Calling a function never changes anything until the declaration has been read; declaring alone produces no output.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l090", "<p class=\"summary\">Practises writing and calling your own functions in the Stanford Karel robot simulator: reach the far corner of a 5x5 world, draw a diagonal line of beepers using a reusable function, and optionally build a chessboard pattern.</p>\n<h3>The Karel environment</h3>\n<ul><li>Karel is a simple robot in an online editor; set the world size to 5 x 5 for a smaller grid.</li><li>Code placed in function main() runs when Run is pressed; reset returns Karel to the start between runs.</li><li>Built-in commands: move() steps one square forward in the facing direction, turnLeft() rotates 90 degrees left, putBeeper() drops a marker on the square, pickBeeper() removes one.</li><li>Each command is a function call, so it needs brackets and a semicolon.</li><li>You can define extra functions outside main and call them from main; the spelling at the call must match the declaration exactly.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> If no turnRight() is available in your Karel editor, define one as three turnLeft() calls.</li></ul>\n<h3>Using functions to avoid repetition</h3>\n<ul><li>A repeated group of commands (four move() calls in a row) can be extracted into a named function such as moveFourTimes(), then called in several places.</li><li>Benefits: shorter code, and bugs are easier to localise because a wrong step count lives in one function.</li><li>Example demo function goInCircle() combines move() and turnLeft() calls and is called from main; calling it twice completes a full loop.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Challenge 1: bottom-left to top-right corner using a helper</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function moveFourTimes() {\n    move();\n    move();\n    move();\n    move();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n    moveFourTimes();   // along the bottom row\n    turnLeft();\n    moveFourTimes();   // up the right column\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Challenge 2: diagonal of five beepers with a repeated unit</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function diagonalMoveAndBeeper() {\n    move();\n    turnLeft();\n    move();\n    putBeeper();\n    turnRight();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n    putBeeper();             // starting corner\n    diagonalMoveAndBeeper();\n    diagonalMoveAndBeeper();\n    diagonalMoveAndBeeper();\n    diagonalMoveAndBeeper(); // 5 beepers in total\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Karel challenges</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Karel editor via the link in the lesson resources, world set to 5 x 5, main() emptied.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Three tasks of increasing difficulty using functions.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Challenge 1: write code moving Karel from the bottom-left to the top-right corner. Any route counts; one route is four moves, turn left, four moves.</li><li>Refactor the repeated move() calls into moveFourTimes() and call it twice.</li><li>Challenge 2: make Karel place a diagonal of five beepers from bottom-left to top-right, with as little repeated code as possible: put the repeating unit (move, turnLeft, move, putBeeper, turn right) into a named function and call it four times after the first putBeeper().</li><li>Optional challenge 3: create a chessboard pattern of alternating beeper squares (solution is in the lesson resources).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Karel ends in the top-right corner; a diagonal line of five beepers; optionally an alternating chessboard of beepers.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function diagonalMoveAndBeeper() {\n    move();\n    turnLeft();\n    move();\n    putBeeper();\n    turnRight();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n    putBeeper();             // starting corner\n    diagonalMoveAndBeeper();\n    diagonalMoveAndBeeper();\n    diagonalMoveAndBeeper();\n    diagonalMoveAndBeeper(); // 5 beepers in total\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Misspelling a function name between its declaration and call.</li><li>Forgetting that Karel keeps facing the new direction after turnLeft(), so the order of moves matters.</li><li>Forgetting the first putBeeper() before the repeated unit, giving only four beepers.</li><li>Reference repo has no Karel code for this lesson; the chess-board solution is given in the next article lecture.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l091", "<p class=\"summary\">A resource lecture that supplies the model solution for the optional Karel chessboard challenge. The article body is only reachable via an Open button and could not be read, so this note records the challenge and an own-words approach.</p>\n<h3>Chessboard challenge approach</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Goal from the previous lecture: fill a 5 x 5 world so beepers sit on alternating squares like a chessboard.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Break the problem into reusable functions: one for a row that starts with a beeper, one for a row that starts empty, and a helper to return to the left edge and move up a row.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Define turnRight() as three turnLeft() calls, since the basic command set only turns left.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Alternating rows start with opposite parity, so call the two row functions in turn.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Building blocks for a chessboard (own illustrative code, not the lesson's)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function turnRight() {\n    turnLeft();\n    turnLeft();\n    turnLeft();\n}\n\n// (Extra) one possible approach: lay a row of alternating beepers, then\n// reposition to the next row and shift the starting offset by one.\nfunction beeperRowStartingWithBeeper() {\n    putBeeper();\n    move();\n    move();\n    putBeeper();\n    move();\n    move();\n    putBeeper();\n}\n\nfunction beeperRowStartingEmpty() {\n    move();\n    putBeeper();\n    move();\n    move();\n    putBeeper();\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Karel chessboard (optional)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Karel 5 x 5 world with main() emptied.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Produce a chessboard pattern of alternating beeper squares using functions.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Write a function that lays one row with beepers on alternate squares.</li><li>Write a variant that starts one square later.</li><li>Write helpers to return to the left edge and move up one row.</li><li>Call row functions alternately for five rows.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Alternating beepers across all five rows.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Article body not read; verify against the lesson's own solution before relying on this.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l092", "<p class=\"summary\">Short admin note placed before the lesson on function parameters. The article content sits behind an Open button and could not be read, so no detail is recorded.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Content not captured; likely a heads-up about the next lesson (function parameters and the Life in Weeks exercise).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-life-in-weeks", "<p class=\"summary\">Extends functions so they accept inputs: a parameter named in the declaration receives the argument passed at the call. The exercise changes getMilk to take money, compute how many bottles it buys and round down with Math.floor.</p>\n<h3>Parameters and arguments</h3>\n<ul><li>The simplest functions have empty brackets; a function with input puts a name between the brackets (a parameter), e.g. function getMilk(bottles) { ... }.</li><li>Inside the body the parameter behaves like a variable holding whatever value the caller supplied.</li><li>At the call the value in the brackets is the argument: getMilk(2) binds 2 to bottles for that run.</li><li>Using the parameter in the body lets one function produce different results, such as logging a message with the number of bottles or computing a cost (2 x 1.5 = 3).</li><li>Demo: change the buy line to console.log(&quot;buy &quot; + bottles + &quot; bottles of milk&quot;) and call getMilk(12); the output includes 12 from the argument.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The terms are often mixed up: parameter = placeholder in the definition, argument = actual value at the call.</li></ul>\n<h3>Math.floor for rounding down</h3>\n<ul><li>money / 1.5 with 5 gives 3.33..., but a whole number of bottles is needed.</li><li>Math.floor(x) rounds down to the nearest integer, so 3.33 becomes 3, and 4.99 would still become 4.</li><li>Rounding down is correct here because you cannot buy a fraction of a bottle.</li><li>Looking up an unfamiliar task like rounding on a search engine is normal programmer practice.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Math.round rounds to nearest and Math.ceil always rounds up.</li></ul>\n<h3>Console tips</h3>\n<ul><li>Clear the console with Cmd+K (Mac) or Ctrl+K (Windows) before re-running to read fresh output.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Parameter used inside the body</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function getMilk(bottles) {\n    console.log(&quot;leaveHouse&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;buy &quot; + bottles + &quot; bottles of milk&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;enterHouse&quot;);\n}\ngetMilk(12);   // argument 12 is bound to bottles</pre>\n<h2>The task: getMilk takes money</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The getMilk function from the previous lesson (code downloadable from the lesson resources).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Change the function to accept an amount of money and print how many whole bottles of milk can be bought at 1.5 per bottle.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Rename the parameter to money.</li><li>Inside the function compute var numberOfBottles = money / 1.5.</li><li>Notice 5 gives 3.33; wrap the division in Math.floor() to round down.</li><li>Log &quot;buy &quot; + numberOfBottles + &quot; bottles of milk&quot; in place of the fixed message.</li><li>Call getMilk(5) and confirm it reports 3.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> getMilk(5) logs buy 3 bottles of milk.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function getMilk(money) {\n    console.log(&quot;leaveHouse&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveRight&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveRight&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveUp&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveUp&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveUp&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveUp&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveRight&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveRight&quot;);\n    var price = 1.5;\n    var bottles = Math.floor(money / price);\n    console.log(&quot;buy &quot; + bottles + &quot; bottles of milk&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveLeft&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveLeft&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveDown&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveDown&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveDown&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveDown&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveLeft&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;moveLeft&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;enterHouse&quot;);\n}\ngetMilk(5);   // buy 3 bottles of milk</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Dividing without rounding gives fractional bottles.</li><li>Forgetting a space when concatenating, e.g. &quot;of milk&quot; glued to the number (a slip visible in the demo's first attempt).</li><li>Calling a function that expects an argument with empty brackets leaves the parameter undefined.</li><li>Reference code uses a variable price rather than a literal 1.5; same behaviour.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l094", "<p class=\"summary\">Walks through the Life in Weeks exercise: take an age as a function parameter, assume a lifespan of 90 and fixed unit counts, compute remaining days, weeks and months, and log an exact sentence built by concatenation.</p>\n<h3>Exercise logic</h3>\n<ul><li>The function lifeInWeeks(age) receives the current age as its parameter.</li><li>Assumptions given by the exercise: 365 days, 52 weeks and 12 months per year, and a lifespan of 90 years.</li><li>Compute yearsRemaining = 90 - age first, then multiply by 365, 52 and 12 to get days, weeks and months left.</li><li>Build the output sentence by breaking the string at each placeholder and concatenating the numbers with +.</li><li>The automated checker compares the text exactly, so spacing, capitalisation, commas and the full stop must match the sample output.</li></ul>\n<h3>Verifying</h3>\n<ul><li>Test with a known age: age 12 gives 78 years left, so 28470 days, 4056 weeks and 936 months.</li><li>The exercise runs in an online editor with a check-solution button; the code body goes inside the provided function and the call passes an actual age.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution in the lecture's approach</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function lifeInWeeks(age) {\n    var yearsRemaining = 90 - age;\n    var days = yearsRemaining * 365;\n    var weeks = yearsRemaining * 52;\n    var months = yearsRemaining * 12;\n    console.log(&quot;You have &quot; + days + &quot; days, &quot; + weeks + &quot; weeks, and &quot; + months + &quot; months left.&quot;);\n}\nlifeInWeeks(12);   // You have 28470 days, 4056 weeks, and 936 months left.</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo version (same results, different arrangement)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function lifeInWeeks(age) {\n    var days = 365, weeks = 52, months = 12, yearsOld = 90;\n    var remainingDays = (yearsOld * days) - (days * age);\n    var remainingWeeks = (yearsOld * weeks) - (weeks * age);\n    var remainingMonths = (yearsOld * months) - (months * age);\n    console.log(&quot;You have &quot; + remainingDays + &quot; days, &quot; + remainingWeeks + &quot; weeks, and &quot; + remainingMonths + &quot; months left.&quot;);\n}\nlifeInWeeks(18);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Life in Weeks</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Online coding exercise with an empty lifeInWeeks(age) function.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Log exactly: You have X days, Y weeks, and Z months left. for a lifespan of 90.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create yearsRemaining as 90 minus age.</li><li>Compute days, weeks and months by multiplying by 365, 52 and 12.</li><li>console.log the sentence, concatenating the three values.</li><li>Call lifeInWeeks with a test age and compare the output text exactly.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> lifeInWeeks(12) logs You have 28470 days, 4056 weeks, and 936 months left.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function lifeInWeeks(age) {\n    var yearsRemaining = 90 - age;\n    var days = yearsRemaining * 365;\n    var weeks = yearsRemaining * 52;\n    var months = yearsRemaining * 12;\n    console.log(&quot;You have &quot; + days + &quot; days, &quot; + weeks + &quot; weeks, and &quot; + months + &quot; months left.&quot;);\n}\nlifeInWeeks(12);   // You have 28470 days, 4056 weeks, and 936 months left.</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Typos or wrong punctuation in the sentence fail the exact-match checker.</li><li>Reference repo calls lifeInWeeks(18) and subtracts totals instead of computing years first; equivalent maths.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Ages over 90 give negative results; not handled.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-functions-3", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers functions that send a value back with return, capturing that value in a variable, and splitting work into small functions (calcBottles, calcChange) whose outputs feed other code. Ends by making the price a parameter too.</p>\n<h3>The return keyword</h3>\n<ul><li>A function can take input (parameters) and give output (a return value); this is the third kind of function after no-input and with-input.</li><li>Place return followed by an expression as the last statement; the value becomes the result of the call expression.</li><li>Capture it by assigning the call to a variable: var change = getMilk(4); the right-hand side is replaced by the returned value.</li><li>Without a return statement the call only runs the body and produces no useful value (it evaluates to undefined).</li><li>Demo: return money % 1.5 gives the leftover cash; 4 with bottles at 1.5 gives 1, 3 gives 0.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> return also ends the function immediately, so nothing after it in the same path runs.</li></ul>\n<h3>Composing functions</h3>\n<ul><li>Move a self-contained calculation into its own function, e.g. calcBottles(startingMoney, costPerBottle) that returns the number of bottles.</li><li>A function call can be used inside another expression, e.g. concatenated in a log string; when execution reaches it the engine runs the function and substitutes the returned value.</li><li>Arguments are matched by position: first argument to first parameter, second to second.</li><li>Do the same for change: calcChange(startingAmount, costPerBottle) returns startingAmount % costPerBottle, and getMilk can return calcChange(...) as its output.</li><li>Print the result with a message by calling getMilk inside console.log and concatenating a string.</li><li>Making cost a parameter of getMilk as well removes the hard-coded 1.5: getMilk(10, 3) works with new prices without editing the maths.</li></ul>\n<h3>Modulo recap</h3>\n<ul><li>Modulo gives the remainder, so spending on whole bottles leaves money % price as change, e.g. 5 with price 1.5 gives 0.5.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Functions with outputs working together</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function calcBottles(startingMoney, costPerBottle) {\n    var numberOfBottles = Math.floor(startingMoney / costPerBottle);\n    return numberOfBottles;\n}\n\nfunction calcChange(startingAmount, costPerBottle) {\n    var change = startingAmount % costPerBottle;\n    return change;\n}\n\nfunction getMilk(money, costPerBottle) {\n    console.log(&quot;leaveHouse&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;buy &quot; + calcBottles(money, costPerBottle) + &quot; bottles of milk&quot;);\n    console.log(&quot;enterHouse&quot;);\n    return calcChange(money, costPerBottle);\n}\n\nvar change = getMilk(5, 1.5);   // logs &quot;buy 3 bottles of milk&quot;\nconsole.log(&quot;Hello master, here is your $&quot; + change + &quot; change.&quot;);   // $0.5\ngetMilk(10, 3);                 // 3 bottles, 1 change</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo version (more explicit variables)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var money = 4;\nvar price = 1.5;\n\nfunction calcBottles(money, price) {\n    var bottles = Math.floor(money / price);\n    return bottles;\n}\nvar bottles = calcBottles(money, price);\n\nfunction calcChange(money, price) {\n    var change = money % price;\n    return change;\n}\nvar change = calcChange(money, price);\n\nfunction getMilk(change, bottles) {\n    return &quot;The price of each bottle of milk is $&quot; + price + &quot;. You gave me $&quot; + money + &quot;, so you can purchase &quot; + bottles + &quot; bottles of milk. Your change will be $&quot; + change;\n}\nconsole.log(getMilk(change, bottles));</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Calling a function and ignoring its return value when you needed it.</li><li>Forgetting return so the variable becomes undefined.</li><li>Swapping argument order when a function has several parameters.</li><li>Floating point output such as 0.5 change is fine here but decimals can show tiny errors in other calculations.</li><li>Reference repo diverges: it passes change and bottles into getMilk and builds a message rather than returning change; also leaves a non-English variable name, which was renamed in the snippet above.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s14-bmi", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the interactive BMI calculator challenge: write a bmiCalculator function that takes weight then height and returns the BMI, then use the Check Solution button. The walkthrough comes in the next lecture.</p>\n<h3>Challenge requirements</h3>\n<ul><li>BMI formula: weight divided by height squared, with weight in kilograms and height in metres.</li><li>The function must be named bmiCalculator and return the BMI so that a line such as var bmi = bmiCalculator(weight, height); stores the result.</li><li>Parameter order matters: weight first, then height, to match the provided calling line.</li><li>Workflow: copy the starter code from index.js into a DevTools Snippet (or any JS runner), write the function, console.log the bmi variable and compare with an online BMI calculator.</li><li>When satisfied, press Check Solution in the exercise page; the solution video follows in the next lecture.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo solution (the lecture itself shows no code)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function bmiCalculator(weight, height) {\n    var bmi = weight / Math.pow(height, 2);\n    return Math.round(bmi);\n}\nvar bmi = bmiCalculator(60, 1.7);\nconsole.log(&quot;Your bmi is &quot; + bmi);</pre>\n<h2>The task: BMI calculator function</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter index.js with the calling line var bmi = bmiCalculator(weight, height); in the exercise page.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create bmiCalculator(weight, height) returning the BMI.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Declare function bmiCalculator with parameters weight then height.</li><li>Compute weight / height squared (Math.pow(height, 2) or height * height).</li><li>Return the value (the reference solution rounds with Math.round).</li><li>Log the result and compare with an online BMI calculator.</li><li>Press Check Solution.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> For example 65 kg and 1.8 m gives about 20 when rounded.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function bmiCalculator(weight, height) {\n    var bmi = weight / Math.pow(height, 2);\n    return Math.round(bmi);\n}\nvar bmi = bmiCalculator(60, 1.7);\nconsole.log(&quot;Your bmi is &quot; + bmi);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Writing weight / height * height cancels out; the squared height needs grouping or Math.pow.</li><li>Passing arguments in the wrong order.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The exercise says nothing about rounding in this video; the reference repo rounds to a whole number.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l097", "<p class=\"summary\">Walkthrough of the BMI calculator function: first verify the formula by hand, then build bmiCalculator(weight, height), then refine it with Math.pow and Math.round.</p>\n<h3>Plan the maths before the code</h3>\n<ul><li>BMI = weight in kg divided by height in metres squared. Work an example on a calculator first (65 kg, 1.8 m: 1.8 x 1.8 = 3.24, 65 / 3.24 is about 20.06) so you know what the function should output.</li><li>The challenge's starter code (variables plus a console.log call) is pasted into a DevTools Snippet, and the function is written beneath it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Building the function</h3>\n<ul><li>Start with the function keyword, then the exact name required by the tests (bmiCalculator), then parentheses holding the inputs in the required order: weight first, height second.</li><li>Inside the curly braces compute bmi = weight / (height * height). The brackets force the multiplication to be evaluated first; without them division would happen first and the result would be wrong.</li><li>Use the return keyword to hand the value back to the caller. Without return the function gives back undefined.</li><li>Paste into the exercise's test runner and press Check solution to confirm it passes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Refinement 1: Math.pow</h3>\n<ul><li>Math.pow(base, exponent) raises a number to a power, so Math.pow(height, 2) replaces height * height. Same result, slightly tidier.</li><li>The method was found by searching the web ('raise a number to a power javascript') and reading the MDN page. Looking things up like this is a normal part of programming.</li></ul>\n<h3>Refinement 2: Math.round</h3>\n<ul><li>Raw output has many decimal places (about 20.0672839...). BMI is normally shown as a whole number.</li><li>Math.round(x) returns the nearest integer: 2.5 and above rounds up to 3, 2.4 rounds to 2. Math.floor always rounds down, so round is the more accurate choice here.</li><li>Change the return line to return Math.round(bmi). With 65 and 1.8 the output is 20, and the test suite still passes.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Math.ceil rounds up and toFixed(n) returns a string with n decimals; neither is needed here.</li></ul>\n<h3>What comes next</h3>\n<ul><li>The following section covers control flow, which is how this BMI function will later be extended to interpret the number.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function bmiCalculator(weight, height) {\n  var bmi = weight / Math.pow(height, 2);\n  return Math.round(bmi);\n}\n\nvar bmi = bmiCalculator(65, 1.8);\nconsole.log(&quot;Your bmi is &quot; + bmi);   // Your bmi is 20</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">First version using multiplication</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function bmiCalculator(weight, height) {\n  var bmi = weight / (height * height);\n  return bmi;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: BMI calculator function (solution)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter code declaring weight and height variables and logging the result of bmiCalculator, with the function left to write.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Define bmiCalculator(weight, height) that returns the BMI as a whole number.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Work an example by hand to know the expected value.</li><li>Write function bmiCalculator(weight, height) { ... } with inputs in that order.</li><li>Compute bmi = weight / (height * height), keeping the brackets.</li><li>Return bmi, then check against the tests.</li><li>Optionally swap in Math.pow(height, 2).</li><li>Return Math.round(bmi) to get a whole number.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> bmiCalculator(65, 1.8) returns 20 and the check passes.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var weight = 60;\nvar height = 1.7;\n\nfunction bmiCalculator(weight, height) {\n  var bmi = weight / Math.pow(height, 2);\n  return Math.round(bmi);\n}\nvar bmi = bmiCalculator(weight, height);\n\nconsole.log(&quot;Your bmi is &quot; + bmi);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing weight / height * height: it evaluates left to right and cancels out.</li><li>Forgetting return, so the function yields undefined.</li><li>Putting parameters in the wrong order (height, weight).</li><li>Using Math.floor when nearest rounding is wanted.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l098", "<p class=\"summary\">A short motivational tip about realistic expectations: coding is neither trivial nor reserved for geniuses, and progress comes from accumulated practice.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Claims that programming is easy apply only to the first basics; real mastery takes sustained effort.</li><li>Finding it difficult is normal and says nothing about ability.</li><li>The opposite myth, that only exceptional people can learn it, is also false.</li><li>Working developers differ from learners mainly in hours of practice, so more work leads to better results.</li><li>Take the middle view: it is hard but achievable, so keep going.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Reading difficulty as a sign you are not suited to coding.</li></ul>");
